  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "A sequence of numbers starts with \\(1,\\) \\(2,\\) and \\(3.\\) The fourth number of the sequence is the sum of the previous three numbers in the sequence: \\(1 + 2 + 3 = 6.\\) In the same way, every number after the fourth is the sum of the previous three numbers. What is the eighth number in the sequence?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(11\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(20\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(37\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(68\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(99\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": "68"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-8-2009-p5.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The sequence begins \\(1,\\) \\(2,\\) \\(3.\\)\n\nThe next terms are \\(1+2+3=6,\\) \\(2+3+6=11,\\) \\(3+6+11=20,\\) \\(6+11+20=37,\\) and \\(11+20+37=68.\\)\n\nThus, D is the correct answer.",
      "hints": [
        "Generate the sequence one term at a time.",
        "Each new term is the sum of the previous three terms."
      ],
